          <section class="model-note card">
            <div class="model-note__header">
              <span>Notes</span>
            </div>
            <ul class="tmm-note-list">
              <li id="pr-note-text">
                Place a point dipole at height <em>z</em> in a stratified stack and read out its
                <strong>local density of optical states</strong> (LDOS) &mdash; the normalized
                spontaneous-emission / decay rate relative to vacuum (the Purcell factor). The
                <em>LDOS vs position</em> view sweeps the emitter through the structure; the
                <em>k&parallel; map</em> resolves the emission over in-plane momentum, exposing
                radiative, evanescent and guided/plasmonic channels (guided modes appear as bright poles).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Editing layers.</strong> Set each layer's thickness, <em>n</em> and &kappa;.
                <strong>+</strong> duplicates a layer below, <strong>&uarr;/&darr;</strong> reorder it,
                <strong>&times;</strong> removes it. Click a layer's colour circle to <em>lock</em> its
                group so edits apply to all members at once. The semi-infinite substrate (below, z&lt;0)
                and superstrate (above) may be absorbing (e.g. a metal mirror); the emitting medium
                itself must be transparent.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Near an absorber.</strong> The emitter is held 1&nbsp;nm clear of any
                absorbing interface, and the sweep leaves that band blank rather than plotting
                through it. Approaching an absorber the LDOS diverges as 1/<em>d</em><sup>3</sup>
                &mdash; almost all of it non-radiative quenching into the metal, not useful emission
                &mdash; so the numbers there grow without bound and the momentum integral behind
                them stops converging. A local, macroscopic description is in any case the wrong
                model at that range: below roughly a nanometre nonlocal and quantum corrections
                dominate, and this model overestimates the quenching.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Method.</strong> Electric, magnetic and magnetoelectric LDOS from a stable
                <em>S</em>-matrix (Redheffer star product) with a contour integral of the dyadic Green
                tensor over in-plane momentum, following Amos &amp; Barnes. This is an in-browser
                TypeScript port of <strong>PyRAMIDS</strong>, validated against it to &lt;1&times;10<sup>&minus;3</sup>.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

